Find the lengths marked $x$ and $y$. The boundary has right-angle turns. The diagram is not to scale.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(4.8,0)--(4.8,4.2)--(3.6,4.2)--(3.6,2.8)--(1.5,2.8)--(1.5,1.2)--(0,1.2)--cycle;
\path (0,0)--(4.8,0) node[midway,below=5pt] {$x$};
\path (4.8,0)--(4.8,4.2) node[midway,right=5pt] {$28\text{ m}$};
\path (4.8,4.2)--(3.6,4.2) node[midway,above=5pt] {$6\text{ m}$};
\path (3.6,4.2)--(3.6,2.8) node[midway,left=5pt] {$9\text{ m}$};
\path (3.6,2.8)--(1.5,2.8) node[midway,above=5pt] {$11\text{ m}$};
\path (1.5,2.8)--(1.5,1.2) node[midway,left=5pt] {$y$};
\path (1.5,1.2)--(0,1.2) node[midway,above=5pt] {$8\text{ m}$};
\path (0,1.2)--(0,0) node[midway,left=5pt] {$7\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The three horizontal steps add to the base.
$\begin{aligned}x&=8+11+6\\&=25\text{ m}\end{aligned}$
The three vertical steps add to the right-hand side.
$7+y+9=28$
$\begin{aligned}y&=28-(7+9)\\&=12\text{ m}\end{aligned}$
